---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getSiteSettings } from "../../lib/sanity.js";

const settings = await getSiteSettings();
---
<Base
  title="About | hredd.org"
  description="Why hredd.org exists, its editorial position, and how to contribute."
  activePage="about"
>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom:clamp(36px,4vw,56px)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker reveal">About</p>
      <h1 class="display reveal" data-delay="1" style="font-size:clamp(40px,5.6vw,80px)">Tracked from the side<br />that answers the <em>questionnaires</em></h1>
      <p class="standfirst reveal" data-delay="2" style="margin-top:26px">
        hredd.org tracks global human rights and environmental due diligence
        legislation and its implications for those most affected and least
        consulted. Independent. Monthly. Published from Noida, India, since 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap" style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr)); gap:24px">
      <div class="panel reveal">
        <h4>Why we exist</h4>
        <p style="margin:0; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          HREDD law is written in Brussels, Washington and Canberra, but it
          lands on factory floors and farm gates in the Global South. We read
          every regime from the receiving end.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel reveal" data-delay="1">
        <h4>How we work</h4>
        <p style="margin:0; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          Eighteen laws, reviewed monthly, with a changelog on every entry.
          No paywalls and no consulting upsell: a public ledger of who must
          do what, and by when, with a primary source behind every claim.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel reveal" data-delay="2">
        <h4>The editorial position</h4>
        <p style="margin:0; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          This publication supports mandatory due diligence and is impatient
          with how it is currently done. Disclosure without consequences has
          failed, yet compliance designed without supplier knowledge produces
          burden without protection, and both failures deserve documentation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel reveal" data-delay="3">
        <h4>The editor</h4>
        <p style="margin:0; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          Vir Mehta is a decent work and supply chain sustainability
          practitioner based in Noida, India. He has spent his career on the
          supplier side of agricultural and garment supply chains, authored
          the first practitioner-facing CSDDD self-assessment toolkit, and
          writes here in a personal capacity.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel reveal" data-delay="1">
        <h4>Contribute</h4>
        <p style="margin:0; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          Field reports, analysis and corrections are welcome, particularly
          from practitioners writing about laws as they land in producing
          countries. Pitches should be short, specific and grounded in
          first-hand knowledge of a supply chain.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel reveal" data-delay="2" id="contact">
        <h4>Contact</h4>
        <p style="margin:0 0 18px; color:var(--ink-dim)">
          Corrections, tips, pitches and field reports are welcome. Every
          message is read, though replies can take a week during field travel.
        </p>
        <a class="btn" href={`mailto:${settings.contactEmail}`}><span>Write to us</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
